WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.130
From skill to paid work: discovery, pricing and reporting.

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.720
This is the video for module three of the professional SEO course.

3
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:19.530
Discovery is where SEO projects succeed or fail. If you do not know which enquiries make the client money, you will optimise the wrong pages.

4
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:28.460
In this video you will learn to ask the right questions, get safe access, price your work clearly, and report in plain language.

5
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:37.000
First, goals. What does a good month look like, and how do enquiries arrive? By form, phone, WhatsApp or walk-in?

6
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:45.190
Second, customers. Ask for five recent enquiries, with personal details removed, and note their words.

7
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:57.620
Third, history. For example, Blessing's logistics firm in Port Harcourt moved to a new domain and nobody set up redirects. That became the first thing to investigate.

8
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:09.000
Then competitors, checked with your own phone search, and constraints. Send a one-page brief within two working days, and ask the client to confirm it in writing.

9
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:21.150
Owners can add you with your own Google account. For most audits, ask for full user. Ownership stays with the client, so they never lose control if you stop working together.

10
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:38.960
Never ask for a password. Data can include names and phone numbers, and the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 governs it. Request only what you need, protect it with two-step verification, and delete copies when the project ends.

11
00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:50.660
There is no official rate, so research your market. Ngozi chose an illustrative rate of 10,000 naira an hour. She adds up her audit tasks: 20 hours.

12
00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.770
20 hours times 10,000 is 200,000 naira.

13
00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:05.020
She adds 15% contingency for surprises, like a messy domain history. That makes 230,000 naira.

14
00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:21.800
To implement the plan, she offers a retainer of 16 hours a month, 160,000 naira, with a 3-month minimum of 480,000 naira. She asks for 50% before work starts and 50% on delivery.

15
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:30.300
Every number traces back to hours and a rate, so if the client wants a discount, she can reduce scope instead of just cutting the price.

16
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:42.840
Scope creep arrives politely. The bookshop owner asks Ngozi to also review 15 blog posts. She answers warmly, in writing, and says it is outside the agreed scope.

17
00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:48.740
She estimates about 4 hours, which is 40,000 naira at the agreed rate.

18
00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:59.180
She offers to add it now, swap it for something in the current scope, or leave it for later. The owner chooses later, and Ngozi records the decision.

19
00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:11.820
In every proposal, never promise positions, and never include bought links or automated content. Google's spam policies prohibit them, and the client's site carries the risk.

20
00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:22.250
An owner reads for three things: what is wrong, what it costs, and what happens next. So lead with a one-page summary he can read on his phone.

21
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:32.070
Remove the jargon, but keep the numbers. Say your main service pages take almost five seconds to show on a phone, not mobile LCP is poor.

22
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:43.410
Zainab targets logistics firms in Rivers State. She plans to contact 80 businesses, each with a personal note naming one issue she saw on their site.

23
00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:49.520
Never send bulk unsolicited messages or add people to WhatsApp groups without consent.

24
00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:59.280
Using her own planning assumptions, 15% reply, half of those book a call, and one in three accepts. That is 2 new clients.

25
00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:08.280
At week 8 she compares her real numbers with the plan, and changes her message or niche if replies are far below 12.

26
00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:24.860
To remember. Confirm a written brief before you price. Keep ownership with the client. Price from hours and a rate. Handle extras with a written change request. And report in plain language, with case studies shared only with permission.

27
00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:34.750
Now practise with a real small business. Run a discovery call, send a brief, price your audit, write your summary and plan your outreach.

28
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.050
Continue with the lessons below.
